{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Regime bias: model underpredicts during sustained uptrends/rallies (≈ +1 to +3 EUR vs realized for ~days 1–15 in Examples 1–3). Apply a positive level shift when last 5–10 days show higher highs/higher lows.",
    "Turning-point/mean-reversion error: model is too slow to flip from bullish to bearish; after the peak it stays elevated and misses the selloff timing (Examples 2–5). Introduce earlier mean-reversion when momentum weakens (e.g., if 3–5 day return turns negative after a run-up).",
    "Crash magnitude underestimation: in sharp drawdowns the model remains far too high, especially beyond ~day 20 (Example 4: forecasts ~73–74 while realized falls to ~63–66). Add a downside stress adjustment when volatility expands and recent returns are negative.",
    "Horizon shape issue: forecast paths are too smooth/flat and do not capture the increasing probability of large moves with horizon; errors grow with lead time (notably days 20–30). Increase forecast variance and allow stronger drift changes at longer horizons.",
    "Spike handling: after upside spikes, the model tends to project continued strength rather than the observed fade; cap post-spike continuation and bias toward partial retracement over the next 5–15 days."
  ]
}
